The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=\oint_C P\,\mathrm{d}y-Q\,\mathrm{d}x\\&=\int_0^{2\pi}(P y\prime-Q x\prime)\,\mathrm{d}t.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x(t)&=2 \cos{\left(t \right)},\qquad y(t)=4 \sin{\left(t \right)},\\P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \cos{\left(t \right)},\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=4 \sin{\left(t \right)} + 4 \cos{\left(t \right)},\\x\prime(t)&=- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)},\qquad y\prime(t)=4 \cos{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=\left(2 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(4 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\quad-\left(4 \sin{\left(t \right)} + 4 \cos{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&\qquad\cdot\left(- 2 \sin{\left(t \right)}\right)\\&=8 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+8 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+8 \cos^{2}{\left(t \right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Using $\cos^2t+\sin^2t=1$, simplify the integrand:

$$
\begin{aligned}
P y\prime-Q x\prime&=8 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\\&\quad{}+8.
\end{aligned}
$$

For terms containing a remaining factor $\cos t$, use $u=\sin t$, $\mathrm{d}u=\cos t\,\mathrm{d}t$.

Integrate these terms separately:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 8 \sin{\left(t \right)} \cos{\left(t \right)}\,\mathrm{d}t&=4 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int 8\,\mathrm{d}t&=8 t+C.
\end{aligned}
$$

For the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[4 \sin^{2}{\left(t \right)}\right]_0^{2\pi}&=0\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left[8 t\right]_0^{2\pi}&=16 \pi\\&\quad-\left(0\right)\\&=16 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

Adding the contributions gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi&=(0) + (16 \pi)=16 \pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,

$$
